/* PicoMite override of pico-sdk's script_include/default_text_excludes.incl
   (selected via pico_add_linker_script_override_path in CMakeLists.txt).
   Identical to the SDK fragment except *libm.a: is NOT excluded from FLASH,
   so libm .text stays in FLASH instead of being copied into RAM by the
   .data catch-all (saves ~4 KB of RAM; complex math funcs are the bulk).
   Keep in sync with the SDK original when updating the SDK. */
/* libgcc's complex arithmetic (__divdc3, __muldc3, __divsc3, __mulsc3: 5.5 KB
   on the RP2040) runs only for MMBasic's complex maths and was never seen in a
   profile, so it goes in FLASH too; the rest of libgcc (shifts, the switch
   helpers) is small and hot and stays in RAM.  Placed here, ahead of the line
   that keeps libgcc out of FLASH, so this rule claims those sections first. */
*libgcc.a:_divdc3.o(.text*)
*libgcc.a:_muldc3.o(.text*)
*libgcc.a:_divsc3.o(.text*)
*libgcc.a:_mulsc3.o(.text*)
/* The RP2040's double-precision wrappers (add, subtract, multiply, divide,
   compare, convert) and the 64-bit multiply run in nearly every BASIC
   expression and were the hottest code left in FLASH in a PC-sample profile
   (2026-10-02, Peter): they are left out of FLASH so the .data catch-all puts
   them in RAM (1 KB), where unused functions are still dropped.  Measured on
   the RP2040 VGA at 378 MHz: solar_eclipse and PETSCII Robots ~1% faster.
   The hardware-divider wrappers were tried in RAM too and made things slower
   (benchmark.bas up to 16%), so they stay in FLASH.  (The SDK's
   PICO_DOUBLE_IN_RAM would also have put 3.3 KB of the V1 ROM shim in RAM.)
   The RP2350 does not link these objects. */
*(EXCLUDE_FILE(*libgcc.a: *libc.a: *lib*_a-mem*.o *double_aeabi_rp2040.S.obj *pico_int64_ops_aeabi.S.obj) .text*)
